You upgraded AKS from 1.28 → 1.29, now the important part is:

👉 How do you verify applications + system-assigned identity are working correctly?

Let’s walk through a practical checklist used in enterprises 👇

✅ 1. First: Understand What Actually Happened in Upgrade
During AKS upgrade:
New nodes (1.29) are created
Pods are rescheduled
Old nodes (1.28) are removed

👉 So risks are:
Pods not coming up properly
App compatibility issues
Identity/access failures


🔍 2. POST-UPGRADE CHECKS (VERY IMPORTANT)
🧱 A. Cluster Health Checks
✅ Check nodes
Shellkubectl get nodesShow more lines
✔️ Expect:
All nodes in Ready state
All showing version 1.29

✅ Check system pods
Shellkubectl get pods -n kube-systemShow more lines
✔️ Ensure:
CoreDNS, kube-proxy, CSI drivers running
No CrashLoopBackOff

🚀 B. Application Health Checks
✅ Check all application pods
Shellkubectl get pods -A``Show more lines
✔️ Look for:
STATUS = Running
READY = 1/1, 2/2


✅ Check logs
Shellkubectl logs <pod>Show more lines
✔️ Look for:
No errors after restart
No dependency failures


✅ Describe pod (VERY useful)
Shellkubectl describe pod <pod-name>Show more lines
Look for:
Event errors
Image pull issues
Volume mount issues


🌐 C. Application Functional Testing
✅ Do real validation:
Call APIs (Postman / curl)
Access UI
Run critical workflows

✔️ Example:
curl https://your-app-url/health``

📦 D. Storage & Volume Checks
👉 Very common failure area
✅ Verify PVC
Shellkubectl get pvcShow more lines
✅ Verify mount inside pod
kubectl exec -it <pod> -- ls /mnt/data
✔️ Confirm:
Data accessible
No mount failure


🔐 E. System Assigned Identity Checks (CRITICAL)
This is what you specifically asked 👇

✅ Step 1: Confirm identity still attached
az aks show --name <cluster> --resource-group <rg>
✔️ Check:

identity.type = SystemAssigned


✅ Step 2: Verify RBAC role still exists
Example:
laz role assignment list \  --assignee <principal-id>
✔️ Ensure roles like:

Storage Blob Data Contributor
Key Vault access roles


✅ Step 3: Test from inside pod
If app accesses storage / Key Vault:
Run inside pod:
Shellkubectl exec -it <pod> -- curl <storage or kv endpoint>Show more lines
✔️ Confirm:
No 403 / auth errors


✅ Step 4: Check workload identity / Managed Identity binding
If using:
AAD Pod Identity OR
Workload Identity

Check:
kubectl get azureidentity
kubectl get azureidentitybinding
✔️ Ensure mapping exists

✅ Step 5: Application logs (VERY IMPORTANT)
Look for:
Token failures
MSI endpoint errors
Access denied errors


📊 F. Monitoring & Observability
Since you mentioned Dynatrace earlier 👇
✅ Check metrics:
CPU spike?
Pod restarts?
Latency increase?

✅ Check logs:
Errors increased?
Dependency failures?

✅ Alerts:
Any triggered after upgrade?


✅ 4. SIMPLE CHECKLIST (MEMORIZE THIS)
👉 After AKS upgrade, ALWAYS validate:
✅ Infra
Nodes upgraded
System pods running

✅ Applications
Pods running
Logs clean
APIs working

✅ Storage
Volumes mounted
Data accessible

✅ Identity
Managed identity attached
Role assignments intact
No auth errors in logs

✅ Monitoring
No spike in failures
No alerts
